  "body": "### Change\n\nIf healing removes edges and the healed solid fails the 0.001 mm endpoint limit, the STEP importer checks the original solid. It keeps the original edges only if the source meets that limit and passes the existing integrity and measurement checks and Solidcore's full level 2 validation. The mesher still checks the requested chord tolerance.\n\nThe importer checks endpoint gaps before computing volume and area. It polls for cancellation before rebuilding the source, before level 2 validation and before integration. It integrates only the selected body. When the source exceeds the endpoint limit, the refusal gives the source gap, limit, input-record edge index, vertex coordinates, edge counts and healed gap.\n\n### Results on the reported part\n\nThe patched kernel importer still refuses the Fusion heater block. The source endpoint gap is 0.007736 mm, above the 0.001 mm limit. The worst source endpoint is on input-record edge 647 (zero-based). Healing reduces the edge count from 999 to 998 and increases the maximum gap to 0.011573 mm. The reader does not expose original STEP entity IDs for edges. The input-record index and model-space vertex coordinates locate the measured endpoint.\n\nTwo runs per commit used the same macOS arm64 machine, compiler options, debug dependencies and private STEP file. The order was old, new, new, old. User CPU time for the complete import fell from 15.29–15.62 s on `7fd89ffe9` to 7.42–7.45 s on `a65a65671`. Elapsed time was 18.79–20.55 s and 8.87–8.95 s, respectively. Other builds were running on the host.\n\nThe normal import stops at the gap check. A separate diagnostic ran level 2 directly on the original 345-face solid. The check alone took 0.404 s and reported 11 findings. This single sample does not establish a false-refusal rate.\n\n### Regression tests\n\n- The synthetic box with a 0.0007 mm edge has a source gap of 0.0008 mm. Healing increases it to 0.001063 mm. Recovery preserves all 13 edges and the box meshes at a 0.005 mm chord target.\n- The ordinary healed box keeps 12 edges and has no source-recovery note.\n- A box with an excessive source gap reports the measured gap, the limit, the independently identified edge and vertex, and the healed gap.\n- A source that meets the endpoint limit but fails full geometry validation is refused.\n\n### Verification and limits\n\n- The quick gate against `origin/main` passed all 15 steps and 114 tests on `a65a65671`. All-target kernel Clippy passed with warnings denied.\n- A successful level 2 recovery through the normal importer has been exercised on the synthetic box. The cost and acceptance rate of recovery on other production models are unmeasured. The GUI and MCP were not rerun on this build. The release-mode heavy gate is pending before this draft is marked ready.\n- The fallback applies to STEP exchange. Solidcraft keeps its existing persisted-model import contract. A net loss of edges triggers the fallback. If healing removes an edge and adds another, the count can conceal the loss and the ordinary gap refusal still applies.\n\nThe issue stays open for the decision about viewing a model whose source exceeds machining tolerance.\n\nEffort: high\n\nChangelog: STEP import preserves validated short edges when healing would exceed the endpoint tolerance. An over-limit source refusal locates the worst endpoint and reports both measurements.\n\nRefs #3252\n",
